------------------------------- DERIVATION -------------------------------------
(arithmetic from the instrument numbers above + artifact byte sizes above;
 no new measurements)

Measured z (fraction of down_proj input exactly 0.0):
  BitNet-2B decode pooled mean  z = 0.7888  (810 (token,layer) records,
      27 decode tokens x 30 layers; per-layer decode means 0.6666..0.9075;
      worst single decode record 0.4042, and that is layer 0 only — every
      decode record in L01..L29 is >= 0.58)
  BitNet-2B prefill pooled mean z = 0.7786  (2220 records, 74 tokens)
  M7 silu control decode mean   z = 0.2092  (silu has no exact-zero region;
      this entire fraction is the int8 quantization dead zone, so it is the
      floor the quant grid alone contributes. The relu2-attributable excess
      on BitNet-2B is therefore roughly 0.79 - 0.21 = 0.58 absolute; the
      kernel-visible number, which is what column-skip exploits, is 0.79.)

Down_proj share of decode traffic (BitNet-2B artifact, sizes in header):
  per-layer ternary elements: attn q+o 2x(2560x2560) + k+v 2x(640x2560)
      = 16,384,000; ffn up+gate+down 3x(2560x6912) = 53,084,160;
      total 69,468,160. down_proj = 17,694,720 = 25.5% of layer weights.
  packed at 4 weights/byte: down_proj = 4.42 MB/layer x 30 = 132.7 MB.
  full decode-token weight stream = MODEL.SAF 522.8 MB + EMBED.BIN LM-head
      read 257.3 MB = 780.1 MB -> down_proj = 17.0% of decode traffic.

Column-skip saving (skip whole down_proj columns where input == 0.0;
requires an offline column-major repack of down_proj only, same 2-bit
packing, one column = 2560 weights = 640 contiguous bytes):
  down_proj work and bytes scale by (1-z): at z = 0.7888 that is a
      1/(1-0.7888) = 4.73x reduction of down_proj MACs and weight bytes.
  layer-weight traffic saving: 0.7888 x 25.5% = 20.1%
  whole-decode-token traffic saving incl. LM head: 0.7888 x 17.0% = 13.4%

Break-even: skip machinery per token = zero-test of 6912 inputs + index
build, ~14k simple ops, vs z x 17.7M MACs saved -> overhead ratio ~0.1%,
negligible. The real cost is kernel form: an input-stationary (axpy) column
kernel with per-MAC efficiency penalty epsilon vs the current 4-row LUT dot
kernel breaks even when (1-z)(1+epsilon) < 1, i.e. z > epsilon/(1+epsilon).
  epsilon = 0.25 (realistic)  -> break-even z = 0.20
  epsilon = 1.00 (pessimistic: column kernel half as efficient) -> z = 0.50
Measured z = 0.79 clears the pessimistic break-even with 2.4x headroom on
down_proj kernel time. NOT claimed: any wall-clock speedup (Rule A — needs
a hardware A/B on a named machine), and any prefill/GEMM benefit (batched
column-skip needs the UNION of nonzeros across the batch, which shrinks
fast with batch size — unmeasured).

Contrast with settled negative A6 (do-not-re-propose is respected): A6 was
STATIC WEIGHT zeros (42.21%) exploited by per-element CTZ iteration/gather,
measured 6.59x slower, break-even ~99%. This probe measures DYNAMIC
ACTIVATION zeros (78.9%) exploitable by contiguous whole-column skip with
no per-element gather. Different quantity, different access pattern.
